Nikki knows what it's like when life  enters a downward spiral aged just 14  her mental health began to deteriorate  she sought help from Child and  Adolescent Mental Health Services all  camps I started to self-harm and tear  voices and attempt suicide so then when  I went to my GP they said it's not  serious enough to warrant a cam referral  and they didn't refer me for like  another six months or something so they  just waited for it waiting and waited  till it got to like crisis point Nikki  was ultimately diagnosed with  personality disorder post-traumatic  stress depression and anxiety she now  has the treatment she needs but says  others have not been so lucky I've had  friends that didn't get help at all  because they just wasn't taken seriously  or wasn't bad enough and they they  killed themselves I'll have a chat with  you  contact me this mother wrote an open  letter to the prime minister after  repeated failures by Cam's which she  says could have cost her ten-year-old  daughter her life  it wasn't until things got really bad  that she actually ended up crashing out  of school because school was struggling  with her as well despite their best  efforts that we were referred to an  inpatient unit because they just didn't  know what else to do I would describe  Cam's as being broken falling apart and  needs urgent help an urgent inquiry as  to how they can fix it  Sam has now conducted a survey of more  than a thousand parents who sought Cam's  referrals for their children the survey  found that 66 percent of parents said  they felt let down by cams treatment of  their child seventeen percent were  refused treatment altogether including  one parent whose fourteen-year-old had  taken an overdose and was hospitalized  33 percent said they'd resorted to  seeking private treatment as a result  and 32 percent said they'd been told by  Cam's professionals that their service  had been cut that's been criticised by  the former minister who negotiated the  extra funding at the moment we neglected  horribly about 75% of children and young  people with mental health problems get  no help at all  it's scandalous the government says it  will  it's commitment to invest 1.25 billion  pounds of extra funding in improving  young people's mental health services  the biggest transformation ever in the  sector  last year was having a bad time many  young people like the ones who wrote  this short film have praised the care  they received once they did get  treatment from cams but with continuing  concern that services have been so  underfunded for so long the greatest  worry is for those who don't offer  Hirsch Sky News
 

